{
  "abstract": "We study positive integer sets whose radix expansions are accepted by a finite automaton reading paired digits from the two boundaries. For every odd radix relatively prime to $3$, and every power of $3$, we prove that an infinite such set with zero lower natural density has a fixed positive proportion of one-step Collatz escapes at every sufficiently large cutoff. This density theorem also permits fixed unequal boundary-block lengths. Its proof uses joint real and radix-adic path sets: two successive normalizations turn an incompatible rational affine self-embedding into a product rectangle, while actual-source residue selection excludes components with a constant trailing coordinate. Separately, in every odd radix, a positive-upper-density ordinary folded language with only finitely many closure exceptions contains all sufficiently large members of an arithmetic progression of odd modulus. Two different families of low-digit changes supply accepted ancestors and recover exact target states. Idempotent insertion before a fixed terminal word handles general deterministic automata. Under exact closure the set therefore contains every positive integer prime to $3$, including $1$. Wren's bounded inverse routing and uniform predecessor theorem give at least $c_S N/y$ ancestors of each $y$ prime to $3$ inside the set whenever $N\\ge K_Sy$, with constants depending only on the set. Frozen CPU checks verify arithmetic and automaton interfaces; the universal claims rest on the proofs. The results restrict infinite folded certificates of nonconvergence, but do not exclude finite nontrivial cycles, control individual-orbit descent, treat arbitrary context-free languages, or resolve Collatz.",
